{
  "summary": "P1 credential compromise (KB-029). User Aisha Khan (user_id 5, HR Generalist, aisha.khan@brightline.example, device BL-LT-0105) reports clicking the link in a 'payroll verification' email and entering her password. Exact click time not given by user. Likely matching message in her quarantine: Q-1003 from it-support@brightIine-help.example (lookalike domain, capital I instead of l), subject 'Urgent: verify your payroll login', received 2026-10-10T21:01Z, flagged phishing, not releasable. User may have a copy in her inbox too; told to keep it. Sign-in logs (14 days): 10 sign-ins, all successful, all New York US, corporate (203.0.113.10) or residential (198.51.100.25) IPs, all within usual hours with Duo push; no hosting networks, no failures, no MFA denials. Most recent sign-in is 2026-10-09, before the phishing email arrived, so nothing suspicious seen after the click. Duo enrolled (push, her phone). Actions: reset_password queued (approval id 1). Temp password must go by phone after callback verification. block_sign_in NOT requested because logs show no suspicious activity; reconsider if new sign-ins appear. Still needed: approve reset and call user; keep watching sign-ins; check for mailbox rules/forwarding and revoke sessions if possible; security review of the sender domain, block brightIine-help.example tenant-wide, and check whether other users received or clicked it.",
  "reason": "KB-029 says always escalate; ticket is P1"
}

{
  "outcome": "escalated",
  "reply_to_user": "Hi Aisha, thank you for telling us so quickly. That really helps. We're treating the password you typed as stolen, so we've asked for it to be reset. Once a technician approves the reset, they'll call you to confirm it's you and give you a temporary password over the phone. We'll never send it by email. You'll set a new password the next time you sign in. Our security team is also looking into this now. I checked your recent sign-ins and they all look normal: all from New York and all approved from your own phone. Until the reset is done, please: 1) Don't delete the payroll email, and don't reply to it or forward it. Security may want to look at it. 2) Never approve a Duo push you didn't start yourself. If you get one you didn't expect, deny it and call the help desk right away. 3) If you see anything odd in your mailbox, like emails you didn't send, tell us straight away. Watch for a call from the IT team soon.",
  "internal_note": "P1 phishing credential entry (KB-029). User_id 5, active, not VIP, Duo push enrolled. Sign-in logs, 14 days: 10 sign-ins, all successful, all from NY corporate or residential IPs, all in usual hours with Duo. No hosting networks, failures or MFA denials. Latest sign-in is 2026-10-09, before the phishing message arrived, so nothing suspicious after the click and block_sign_in was not requested. Quarantine holds Q-1003 from it-support@brightIine-help.example (lookalike domain), 'Urgent: verify your payroll login', flagged phishing and not releasable. This is likely the same campaign, and she may have a copy in her inbox. reset_password queued (approval #1): give the temp password by phone after callback verification only. Escalated to Tier 2 (escalation #1) to watch sign-ins, check inbox rules and forwarding, revoke sessions, block the sender domain, and check for other recipients.",
  "kb_articles": [
    "KB-029",
    "KB-008"
  ],
  "confidence": "high"
}
